  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1010 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nModels really strengthen the upper ridge over the Deep South\nThursday night into Friday. Model guidance hints at a very strong \n597 or 598 decameter high pressure center with this feature. This \nupper ridge will produce very strong subsidence over the area and \nshould keep any moderate to high shower/storm chances well to our \nnorth and east of the area. 925 mb temperatures climb to between \n28 and 30 degrees in most guidance by Friday afternoon. This will \nlikely push high temperatures into the 95 to 99 degree range at \nleast with good insolation through the day. A high or two of 100 \ndegrees is not out of the question. It does look like some mixing \nof drier air below 800 mb will occur with the strong subsidence. \nThis will likely lower afternoon dewpoints, maybe more than \ncurrently lowered in straight NBM guidance. For now, lowered \ndewpoints only conservatively for now from NBM (75% NBM/25% \nCONSRAW). Still, heat index values above 105 degrees look like a \ngood bet (though some uncertainty exists if 108 or above heat \nindex values will be met in many locations). However, if NBM \nguidance is correct and less mixing occurs, these values may be \nmore widespread and extreme heat products may be needed for some \nareas. \n\nEither way a heat wave will continue into the weekend, as the \nupper ridge either remain as strong or strengthens a bit more. Some \nguidance on Saturday and Sunday push 925 mb temperatures into the 29 \nto 32 degree range (likely values closer to 32 degrees in NW AL). \nThis could push afternoon highs with strong low level subsidence \ncontinuing over the century mark at more locations. With the \nhighs possibly being a few degrees higher over the weekend, even \nif mixing occurs, extreme heat looks most likely to occur then. \n\nA few models (GFS in particular) try to extend a front into the \nsoutheast Sunday afternoon. However, given the strength of the upper\nridge, this looks very dubious. If that does happen, then highs will\nlikely be a bit less hot and remain in the mid/upper 90s versus \nupper 90s to over 100 degrees. The bigger impact would be a focusing \nof lower level moisture/higher dewpoints over the area Sunday \nafternoon, which would drive heat index values higher despite lower \nhigh temperatures. Whichever solution is correct, heat index values \nlook to still climb to between 105 and 108 degrees at least on Sunday\nafternoon.\n\nBy early next week, models do seem to convergence on a solution that\nslides the center of the upper ridge WNW over Texas and puts the\nTennessee Valley region in a more NW flow aloft. This should bring\nsome increase in afternoon shower/storm activity and likely more\nafternoon cloud cover to the area. 925 mb temperatures will still\nclimb into the 23 to 27 degree though, so highs in the mid 90s at\nleast will likely still continue. However, this drop in highs, may\nmake the oppressive heat less widespread.",
